{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>To make a pleasing fragrance. And the one who offers shall present.</b> Up to the calf [of the golden calf], a sacrifice was a pleasing fragrance even without a grain offering or libations, as in the case of Hevel (Genesis 4:4), of Noah (Genesis 8:21), and of Abraham (Genesis 12:7), and as in: וַיִּשְׁלַח אֶת נַעֲרֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וַיַּעֲלוּ עֹלֹת וַיִּזְבְּחוּ זְבָחִים שְׁלָמִים לַה׳ פָּרִים (Exodus 24:5) — nothing besides this. But when they sinned with the calf, He required a grain offering and libations for the daily burnt offering, which is a public sacrifice; and from the time they sinned with the spies, He required a grain offering and libations to render valid even an individual's sacrifice."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:39:57.185857991Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}